1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:07,246
350 metres below the surface, a wellhead
is being bent back and forth.

2
00:00:07,266 --> 00:00:11,845
Once with every wave, for as long as the
drilling riser is connected.

3
00:00:12,665 --> 00:00:14,922
That's more than 10,000 load cycles a day.

4
00:00:14,942 --> 00:00:17,471
None of them comes close to
breaking the steel.

5
00:00:18,191 --> 00:00:20,753
But every cycle leaves a
little damage behind.

6
00:00:20,773 --> 00:00:24,232
Add up enough of them, and a crack
starts to grow from a weld.

7
00:00:25,053 --> 00:00:28,258
Down here, nobody will ever
inspect those welds.

8
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:33,230
So we have to predict how long they will
last before the rig even arrives.

9
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:38,806
In this video, we'll do exactly that, step by
step, with the method in DNV-RP-C203.

10
00:00:43,433 --> 00:00:44,975
First, a quick refresher.

11
00:00:44,995 --> 00:00:49,847
Fatigue is driven by stress cycles, and what
matters most is the stress range:

12
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:54,844
the difference between the highest and the
lowest stress in a cycle, written Δσ.

13
00:00:55,464 --> 00:00:58,679
In welded steel, cracks almost
always start at a weld.

14
00:00:58,699 --> 00:01:02,094
The weld toe is a sharp notch,
welding leaves tiny flaws,

15
00:01:02,114 --> 00:01:04,310
and residual stresses close to yield.

16
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:09,282
So a crack starts early, and most of the life is
spent growing it through the wall.

17
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:14,734
To measure fatigue strength, we test welded
specimens at a constant stress range

18
00:01:14,754 --> 00:01:16,675
and count the cycles to failure.

19
00:01:16,695 --> 00:01:21,223
Repeat that at several stress ranges, and
plot the results on log-log axes.

20
00:01:21,243 --> 00:01:24,618
They fall around a straight line,
with a lot of scatter.

21
00:01:25,237 --> 00:01:32,593
DNV-RP-C203 gives design S-N curves that sit two
standard deviations below the mean

22
00:01:32,613 --> 00:01:34,104
of the test data.

23
00:01:34,124 --> 00:01:38,102
That's a survival probability
of about 97.7%.

24
00:01:38,723 --> 00:01:40,353
The curve is a power law:

25
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:43,587
the number of cycles to failure
is a constant, ā,

26
00:01:43,607 --> 00:01:46,425
divided by the stress range
to the power m.

27
00:01:46,445 --> 00:01:47,943
For most welds, m is 3.

28
00:01:47,963 --> 00:01:52,365
So if you double the stress range, the
life drops by a factor of 8.

29
00:01:52,385 --> 00:01:55,599
Small errors in stress become
big errors in life.

30
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:00,773
Real loading is nothing like a
constant-amplitude test.

31
00:02:00,793 --> 00:02:04,053
So C203 turns the problem into
a chain of steps.

32
00:02:04,673 --> 00:02:07,303
Describe the sea states the well will see.

33
00:02:07,323 --> 00:02:10,709
Turn each one into a stress
history at every hot spot.

34
00:02:10,729 --> 00:02:12,223
Count the stress cycles.

35
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:13,799
Pick the right S-N curve.

36
00:02:13,819 --> 00:02:16,070
Add up the damage with Miner's rule.

37
00:02:16,090 --> 00:02:19,476
And finally, check it against a
design fatigue factor.

38
00:02:20,096 --> 00:02:22,630
Every link in that chain can
change the answer,

39
00:02:22,650 --> 00:02:25,836
so let's go through them one at a
time, on a real wellhead.

40
00:02:27,667 --> 00:02:28,575
Here's our well:

41
00:02:28,595 --> 00:02:33,275
the same template, conductor and wellhead
housings we modelled in our last video.

42
00:02:33,295 --> 00:02:36,293
The riser pulls on the top of the
blowout preventer,

43
00:02:36,313 --> 00:02:38,440
and that force bends the whole stack.

44
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:41,971
The global beam model shows
where the bending goes.

45
00:02:41,991 --> 00:02:44,558
Most of the moment passes
into the conductor,

46
00:02:44,578 --> 00:02:49,272
and it keeps growing below the seabed, to a peak
about 3 metres below the mudline.

47
00:02:49,893 --> 00:02:53,659
Fatigue cracks start where a high stress
range meets a weak detail.

48
00:02:53,679 --> 00:02:55,862
We'll follow three candidate hot spots:

49
00:02:55,882 --> 00:02:59,592
the weld between the high-pressure
housing and the surface casing,

50
00:02:59,612 --> 00:03:02,813
the weld between the conductor
housing and the conductor,

51
00:03:02,833 --> 00:03:06,316
and a girth weld in the conductor, 3
metres below the mudline,

52
00:03:06,336 --> 00:03:07,955
right where the moment peaks.

53
00:03:09,967 --> 00:03:10,407
Step 1:

54
00:03:10,427 --> 00:03:10,932
the sea.

55
00:03:10,952 --> 00:03:16,188
A scatter diagram tells us how often each
combination of significant wave height

56
00:03:16,208 --> 00:03:17,699
and peak period occurs.

57
00:03:17,719 --> 00:03:19,342
Each cell is a sea state:

58
00:03:19,362 --> 00:03:22,890
a few hours of irregular waves
with steady statistics.

59
00:03:23,510 --> 00:03:27,136
At this site, half the time the
waves are below 2 metres,

60
00:03:27,156 --> 00:03:30,654
and seas above 6 metres occur less
than 2% of the time.

61
00:03:31,274 --> 00:03:34,790
Within a sea state, the surface is a
sum of many sine waves,

62
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:38,149
with the energy spread over
frequency by a wave spectrum.

63
00:03:38,769 --> 00:03:43,366
A global riser analysis turns those waves
into the force at the flex joint,

64
00:03:43,386 --> 00:03:45,336
on top of the blowout preventer.

65
00:03:45,356 --> 00:03:48,784
Here we stand in for it with a
simple transfer function,

66
00:03:48,804 --> 00:03:51,000
so the example stays self-contained.

67
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:54,079
The result is a force history
for every sea state.

68
00:03:56,100 --> 00:03:56,505
Step 2:

69
00:03:56,525 --> 00:03:57,779
from force to stress.

70
00:03:57,799 --> 00:04:01,482
We push the global model with an
increasing flex-joint force,

71
00:04:01,502 --> 00:04:03,849
and record the moment at each hot spot.

72
00:04:03,869 --> 00:04:07,187
These load-to-moment curves are
our transfer functions.

73
00:04:07,807 --> 00:04:09,390
They aren't straight lines.

74
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:14,021
The soil springs soften, the radial clearance
between the two housings closes,

75
00:04:14,041 --> 00:04:17,405
and above about 50 kN the gap to
the template closes too,

76
00:04:17,425 --> 00:04:20,374
and the template starts to carry
part of the load.

77
00:04:20,994 --> 00:04:23,902
So the stress isn't simply
proportional to the force.

78
00:04:23,922 --> 00:04:27,936
We keep the full nonlinear curve, and run
every force history through it.

79
00:04:29,767 --> 00:04:34,721
The beam model gives moments, and a moment
divided by the section modulus gives the

80
00:04:34,741 --> 00:04:35,620
nominal stress.

81
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:37,478
But a weld sees more than that,

82
00:04:37,498 --> 00:04:40,775
because the geometry around it
concentrates the stress.

83
00:04:41,395 --> 00:04:44,649
For the two housing welds, we use
the hot spot method.

84
00:04:44,669 --> 00:04:49,560
In the local solid model, read the surface
stress at half a wall thickness and at

85
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:52,592
one and a half wall thicknesses
from the weld toe,

86
00:04:52,612 --> 00:04:54,774
and extrapolate linearly to the toe.

87
00:04:54,794 --> 00:04:59,807
At the high-pressure housing weld, that gives a
stress concentration factor of 1.6.

88
00:05:00,527 --> 00:05:05,432
At the conductor housing weld, the outside is
actually less stressed than the

89
00:05:05,452 --> 00:05:06,327
nominal value.

90
00:05:06,347 --> 00:05:10,805
The housing thickens inwards, so the
wall's mid-surface steps inwards,

91
00:05:10,825 --> 00:05:13,363
and the axial stress adds local bending.

92
00:05:13,383 --> 00:05:17,329
The inside of the weld is the hot
spot, with a factor of 1.15.

93
00:05:17,349 --> 00:05:19,056
Always check both surfaces.

94
00:05:19,776 --> 00:05:25,254
The conductor girth weld is a standard
detail, so we use the nominal stress,

95
00:05:25,274 --> 00:05:28,509
with a concentration factor
for misalignment.

96
00:05:28,529 --> 00:05:32,705
With 6 mm of hi-lo between the pipe
ends, C203 gives 1.17.

97
00:05:32,725 --> 00:05:38,493
The 0.1t in the formula is misalignment that is
already included in the S-N test

98
00:05:38,513 --> 00:05:38,855
data.

99
00:05:40,667 --> 00:05:41,118
Step 3:

100
00:05:41,138 --> 00:05:42,195
counting cycles.

101
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:44,552
A real stress history is irregular.

102
00:05:44,572 --> 00:05:50,140
Small cycles ride on top of bigger ones, and
it's not obvious what a cycle even is.

103
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,205
The standard answer is rainflow counting.

104
00:05:53,225 --> 00:05:57,654
Reduce the history to its peaks and valleys,
then read them one at a time,

105
00:05:57,674 --> 00:05:59,999
and keep comparing the last two ranges.

106
00:06:00,519 --> 00:06:04,490
Whenever the newest range is at least as
large as the one before it,

107
00:06:04,510 --> 00:06:06,602
the one before it is a closed cycle.

108
00:06:06,622 --> 00:06:09,302
Count it, remove its two
points, and carry on.

109
00:06:09,322 --> 00:06:11,767
Each small cycle is paired off on its own,

110
00:06:11,787 --> 00:06:14,350
without breaking up the big
cycle around it.

111
00:06:14,970 --> 00:06:18,532
Whatever is left at the end is
counted as half cycles.

112
00:06:18,552 --> 00:06:21,850
For a 3-hour sea state, we get
about 1,400 cycles,

113
00:06:21,870 --> 00:06:24,570
sorted into a histogram of stress ranges.

114
00:06:25,190 --> 00:06:28,036
Notice that we count ranges,
not mean stress.

115
00:06:28,056 --> 00:06:30,902
Welds carry residual stresses
close to yield,

116
00:06:30,922 --> 00:06:35,678
so for as-welded joints C203 uses the full
stress range, whatever the mean.

117
00:06:35,698 --> 00:06:40,455
That's also why the lock ring preload and
the weight of the stack drop out.

118
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:42,733
Step 4:

119
00:06:42,753 --> 00:06:43,706
the S-N curve.

120
00:06:43,726 --> 00:06:48,500
C203 has a whole family of curves, from
B1 for plain, unwelded steel,

121
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:52,113
down to W3 for cracks through the
weld metal itself.

122
00:06:52,133 --> 00:06:54,892
Which one applies depends on the detail:

123
00:06:54,912 --> 00:07:00,103
its geometry, how it's welded and inspected,
and which way the stress acts.

124
00:07:00,723 --> 00:07:03,551
Appendix A classifies a long
list of details.

125
00:07:03,571 --> 00:07:07,981
A girth weld made from both sides is a D
curve detail at the weld toe.

126
00:07:08,001 --> 00:07:11,968
And when the hot spot stress comes
from a finite element model,

127
00:07:11,988 --> 00:07:14,373
the D curve is the one to use as well.

128
00:07:14,993 --> 00:07:16,313
Then the environment.

129
00:07:16,333 --> 00:07:20,910
In air, the curve bends at 10 million
cycles, to a shallower slope of 5.

130
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:24,869
In seawater with cathodic protection,
the steep part is lower,

131
00:07:24,889 --> 00:07:27,359
and the bend comes at 1 million cycles.

132
00:07:27,379 --> 00:07:30,104
In free corrosion, there is
no bend at all.

133
00:07:30,124 --> 00:07:33,361
Our welds are in seawater with
cathodic protection.

134
00:07:33,981 --> 00:07:36,405
Thick walls are weaker in fatigue,

135
00:07:36,425 --> 00:07:41,724
so the stress range is scaled by the
thickness over 25 mm, to the power k.

136
00:07:41,744 --> 00:07:45,677
For the 38 mm conductor, that's a
9% penalty on stress.

137
00:07:45,697 --> 00:07:47,762
The thinner casing gets none.

138
00:07:48,381 --> 00:07:49,267
One more thing:

139
00:07:49,287 --> 00:07:52,891
under variable amplitude loading,
there is no fatigue limit.

140
00:07:52,911 --> 00:07:55,187
Cycles below the bend still do damage.

141
00:07:55,207 --> 00:07:57,422
They just follow the shallower slope.

142
00:07:59,233 --> 00:07:59,644
Step 5:

143
00:07:59,664 --> 00:08:00,445
adding it up.

144
00:08:00,465 --> 00:08:05,434
Palmgren-Miner's rule says that each stress
range uses up a fraction of the life:

145
00:08:05,454 --> 00:08:10,053
the number of cycles it occurs, divided by
the number the S-N curve allows.

146
00:08:10,073 --> 00:08:12,579
The damage is the sum of those fractions,

147
00:08:12,599 --> 00:08:15,350
and failure is predicted when
it reaches one.

148
00:08:15,970 --> 00:08:20,174
We do this for every sea state, weight
each one by how often it occurs,

149
00:08:20,194 --> 00:08:22,553
and get the damage per day of operation.

150
00:08:22,573 --> 00:08:25,111
This map shows where the
damage comes from.

151
00:08:25,731 --> 00:08:27,472
The calm days hardly matter.

152
00:08:27,492 --> 00:08:32,755
The waves are below 2 metres half of the time,
and cause less than 1% of the damage.

153
00:08:32,775 --> 00:08:34,830
The storms don't dominate either:

154
00:08:34,850 --> 00:08:39,043
they are rare, and above 50 kN the
template takes part of the load.

155
00:08:39,063 --> 00:08:43,948
Three quarters of the damage comes from
moderate seas, between 3 and 6 metres.

156
00:08:44,667 --> 00:08:46,512
C203 also offers a shortcut.

157
00:08:46,532 --> 00:08:50,574
If the long-term stress ranges follow
a Weibull distribution,

158
00:08:50,594 --> 00:08:53,105
the damage has a closed-form solution.

159
00:08:53,125 --> 00:08:57,566
Fitted to our results, it lands within
10% of the full calculation.

160
00:08:57,586 --> 00:09:02,960
Ignore the bend in the S-N curve, though, and
the life comes out a third shorter.

161
00:09:04,767 --> 00:09:05,208
Step 6:

162
00:09:05,228 --> 00:09:06,327
the design check.

163
00:09:06,347 --> 00:09:09,291
Even with conservative S-N
curves, the loads,

164
00:09:09,311 --> 00:09:12,123
the soil and the fabrication
are uncertain.

165
00:09:12,143 --> 00:09:15,547
So the design standard adds a
design fatigue factor:

166
00:09:15,567 --> 00:09:19,631
the calculated damage, times that
factor, must stay below one.

167
00:09:20,251 --> 00:09:24,287
The factor depends on whether a crack
could be found and repaired,

168
00:09:24,307 --> 00:09:26,254
and on what happens if it isn't.

169
00:09:26,274 --> 00:09:28,221
Typical values run from 1 to 10.

170
00:09:28,241 --> 00:09:31,786
A conductor 3 metres under the mud
can never be inspected,

171
00:09:31,806 --> 00:09:34,675
and losing it threatens the
well, so we use 10.

172
00:09:35,394 --> 00:09:39,653
Here are the results, in days of
riser-connected operation.

173
00:09:39,673 --> 00:09:43,424
The high-pressure housing weld is
far from critical.

174
00:09:43,444 --> 00:09:46,978
The conductor housing weld
allows about 330 days.

175
00:09:46,998 --> 00:09:51,910
And the governing hot spot is the plain
girth weld in the conductor:

176
00:09:51,930 --> 00:09:57,277
1,660 days of calculated life, or 166 days
with the design fatigue factor.

177
00:09:57,998 --> 00:10:03,479
The drilling campaign needs 120 days with the
riser connected, so the well passes,

178
00:10:03,499 --> 00:10:05,157
at about 70% utilisation.

179
00:10:05,177 --> 00:10:07,169
But notice which weld governs.

180
00:10:07,189 --> 00:10:12,537
Not the big forgings, but an ordinary girth
weld, out of sight below the seabed.

181
00:10:14,567 --> 00:10:17,372
Before trusting that answer,
ask what moves it.

182
00:10:17,392 --> 00:10:19,958
Life scales with stress to
the third power,

183
00:10:19,978 --> 00:10:23,024
so 10% more stress costs about a
third of the life.

184
00:10:23,644 --> 00:10:25,828
Fabrication matters just as much.

185
00:10:25,848 --> 00:10:29,636
If the girth weld had 10 mm of
misalignment instead of 6,

186
00:10:29,656 --> 00:10:34,713
the allowable time would drop to 68 days, and
the well would fail the check.

187
00:10:34,733 --> 00:10:37,853
Weld tolerances belong in the
fatigue analysis.

188
00:10:38,473 --> 00:10:39,682
And the environment:

189
00:10:39,702 --> 00:10:43,433
the same weld in free corrosion,
without cathodic protection,

190
00:10:43,453 --> 00:10:45,216
has only about half the life.

191
00:10:45,836 --> 00:10:49,251
So report the result together with the
assumptions that drive it,

192
00:10:49,271 --> 00:10:52,422
and check those assumptions against
what was actually built.

193
00:10:54,233 --> 00:10:55,501
So that's the method:

194
00:10:55,521 --> 00:11:00,161
sea states, stress at the hot spots, rainflow
counting, the right S-N curve,

195
00:11:00,181 --> 00:11:02,675
Miner's sum, and a design fatigue factor.

196
00:11:03,495 --> 00:11:05,628
Every wave leaves a little damage behind.

197
00:11:05,648 --> 00:11:09,041
Count it carefully, and you'll know how
long the steel will last.

